thread:
kevin: FYI: ran into a stream iterator that never unblocks when cancelling the caller ctx during auth refresh. tiny repro attached. wondering whether we should add an explicit Close() or make Next() always honor ctx.Done().

kevin: repro pattern: call Generator.Stream(ctx) then cancel ctx from another goroutine after first token. sometimes Next() blocks indefinitely and Err() stays nil.

kevin: snippet:
```go
ctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(context.Background())
iter := client.Generator().Stream(ctx, req)
go func() {
  for iter.Next() {
    if iter.Value() == "\n" { cancel() }
  }
  log.Println("iterator ended")
}()
// program hangs on exit
```

tara: logs show http2 connection closed but the read loop is stuck in Read() syscall. looks like when auth refresh yields a pause, cancel doesn't always trigger conn.Close().

frank: we've had two approaches previously: 1) add Close()/Abort() to the iterator API to force-close client-side resources, 2) ensure Next() selects on ctx.Done() so it unblocks immediately.

kevin: Close() feels safer since many samples use context.Background() and won't cancel. explicit Close() gives deterministic cleanup.

frank: agreed. implementation detail: iterator hides transport; Close() needs to signal the read pump and close the connection or cancel internal context. also ensure Err() returns context.Canceled.

kevin: I hacked a small wrapper as a stopgap that exposes Close() by creating a child ctx and cancelling it on Close(). works for my repro.

kevin: wrapper:
```go
type CloseIter struct{ it *gen.Iterator; cancel context.CancelFunc }
func (c *CloseIter) Next() bool { return c.it.Next() }
func (c *CloseIter) Value() string { return c.it.Value() }
func (c *CloseIter) Err() error { return c.it.Err() }
func (c *CloseIter) Close() { c.cancel() }
```

